Give status 1, not ferror()'s value, after a builtin's write error.
After running a builtin, evalbltin() ORs ferror(stdout) into its status
to report an output error. ferror() only promises a nonzero value, and
the Microsoft C runtime returns its _IOERR flag, 32. So a builtin such
as "kill -l" or "pwd" whose output went to a closed descriptor or a pipe
with no reader exited with status 32 rather than 1.

Use the truth value of ferror().

---
 shell/ash.c |    3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/shell/ash.c
+++ b/shell/ash.c
@@ -12752,7 +12752,8 @@
 	else
 		status = (*cmd->builtin)(argc, argv);
 	flush_stdout_stderr();
-	status |= ferror(stdout);
+	/* ferror() is any nonzero value on error, not necessarily 1 */
+	status |= (ferror(stdout) != 0);
 	exitstatus = status;
  cmddone:
 	clearerr(stdout);
